<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sher Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">assassinated <a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sher Singh was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> for a short period after the deaths of his predecessors. He was assassinated in 1843 amid factional struggles at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> court. Accounts of the Sikh Empire place his death in the succession of violent deaths that weakened the state before the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sher-singh.jpg" alt="Sher Singh" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ágoston Schoefft · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Maharaja_Sher_Singh_wearing_the_Koh-i-Noor_diamond,_by_August_Schoefft,_ca.1841%E2%80%9342.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sher Singh was born on 4 December 1807, the elder of twins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> and Maharani Mehtab Kaur, and his father's second son; his twin was Tara Singh. In 1820 Ranjit Singh gave him a seat in the Darbar with civil and military honours. He was Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from 1831 to 1834, during which a famine that broke out in 1831 cut the population from 800,000 to 200,000, and in 1834 he commanded the force that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>.</p><p>In 1831 he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> troops against the camp at Balakot of Barelvi, who had declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the Sikhs. The Sikhs closed in from all sides, and about 500 of his followers were killed, among them Shah Ismail Dehlvi; Sher Singh received Rs. 50,000 and a letter of appreciation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In January 1841 Sher Singh, an experienced soldier with the backing of the Sikh Army, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and took the throne after a long siege of the Lahore Fort, ending the regency of Maharani Chand Kaur; thousands died in the siege, and he was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> by the wazir Dhian Singh Dogra. Continuing his father's friendly ties with the British, he let their army retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1842, though many of his officials wished to raid it. He was a patron of the painters Kehar Singh and August Schoefft, followed wrestling and began a communal cleaning of the Amrit Sarovar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> complex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lehna Singh Sandhawalia, Ajit Singh Sandhawalia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhian-singh">Dhian Singh</a> plotted his death, and historians disagree on how he died. In one account, at the Shah Bilawal Baradari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, Ajit Singh asked him to inspect a new shotgun, shot him and beheaded him with his sword. The Sandhawalias also killed Dhian Singh and Sher Singh's son Partap Singh, and father and son were cremated together at the Shah Bilawal Baradari, where both their samadhis stand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sher Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sher Singh</b> was assassinated in 1843, along with the chief minister Dhian Singh, and the five-year-old Duleep Singh was installed under the regency of his mother, Maharani Jind Kaur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Batala, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.82,75.2&amp;year=1843" aria-label="Batala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sher Singh"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3937 4775h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3937 4775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.82,75.2&amp;year=1843">What was here in 1843? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
